    "narrative": "The spotlight today shines squarely on **Corbin Carroll**, who leads our squad with an elite 81.24 efficiency score. Facing Elder, **Corbin Carroll** benefits from a high-upside matchup against a mediocre arm and an advantageous top-of-the-order slot, making him our primary engine for both speed and run production. **Max Muncy** follows closely with a 71.26 mark; despite hitting seventh, he faces a soft matchup against Bradford (xERA 5.29), allowing his raw power metrics to shine through in a favorable park environment. Meanwhile, **Josh Naylor** (64.62), **Gabriel Moreno** (64.15), and **Mickey Moniak** (63.35) provide a high-floor foundation, with **Mickey Moniak** specifically benefiting from a significant 14% platoon boost that elevates his status as a recommended start despite park-factor headwinds.\n\nNavigating the fringes of the lineup remains a tactical necessity given our pursuit of stolen bases. **Jose Caballero** (41.62) and **Zach McKinstry** (41.11) landed just above the \"Below Floor\" threshold; while both are strictly marginal plays today, they remain in the lineup solely for their utility in the scarcest category. **Jose Caballero** faces a steep challenge against an elite arm in Cease, which drastically suppresses his projection, but his ability to swipe a bag keeps him ahead of inactive bench options like **Brice Turang**. The most difficult decision involved keeping **Travis Bazzana** (45.59) and **Cole Young** (45.47) active over reserves; both barely edged out the bench, though they were prioritized over our deep prospect pool\u2014including **Henry Bolte**, **Edwin Arroyo**, **Dalton Rushing**, and **Tyler Soderstrom**\u2014to maximize daily at-bats in a week where we need to close the gap on our competitors.\n\nRisks today are concentrated in the middle-to-bottom of the order. **Austin Riley** (48.70) is trending downward due to a late-order batting position and a challenging matchup against Soroka, and any shift in the lineup order could push him below our 40-point start threshold. We are also monitoring the health of our long-term assets, as **James Wood**, **Trevor Story**, and **Giancarlo Stanton** remain sidelined on the IL, alongside **Vinnie Pasquantino**, leaving our bench thin if any starters are hit by late-afternoon scratches. With no major weather concerns, the focus remains on whether our speed-focused flyers like **Jose Caballero** can manufacture value against high-tier pitching.",
